In the last part, we talked about how to start those conversations, you know, make a good first impression.

151
00:07:22,440 --> 00:07:28,690
But now I'm really curious to explore how to keep those conversations flowing and, you know, make sure they're actually productive.

152
00:07:29,280 --> 00:07:30,340
You know, it's interesting you should say that.

153
00:07:30,380 --> 00:07:33,350
I was just thinking about how it's like building a bridge.

154
00:07:33,630 --> 00:07:38,560
You have the two sides, you and the person you're talking to, and the conversation is what connects them.

155
00:07:39,080 --> 00:07:39,600
I like that.

156
00:07:40,220 --> 00:07:41,770
So how do we build that bridge?

157
00:07:41,990 --> 00:07:44,750
Well, just like with any bridge, you need a strong foundation.

158
00:07:45,070 --> 00:07:47,860
Okay, so what's the foundation of a good conversation?

159
00:07:48,820 --> 00:07:50,240
I'd say active listening.

160
00:07:50,420 --> 00:07:51,060
Active listening.

161
00:07:51,100 --> 00:07:52,050
I hear that term a lot.

162
00:07:52,110 --> 00:07:53,850
Yeah, it's more than just hearing the words.

163
00:07:54,010 --> 00:07:59,520
You know, it's about really understanding the message, both the verbal and the nonverbal cues.

164
00:07:59,880 --> 00:08:01,850
So it's about being present in the moment.

165
00:08:02,100 --> 00:08:04,720
Paying attention to their tone of voice, their body language.

166
00:08:04,780 --> 00:08:05,240
Exactly.

167
00:08:05,280 --> 00:08:08,130
And even the silences between words can tell you a lot.

168
00:08:08,210 --> 00:08:11,510
It's like you're trying to see the world from their perspective.

169
00:08:12,170 --> 00:08:12,810
Precisely.

170
00:08:13,690 --> 00:08:16,780
And that level of engagement helps you build rapport.

171
00:08:17,220 --> 00:08:23,770
And it also allows you to pick up on those subtle cues that can guide the conversation in a more meaningful direction.

172
00:08:24,150 --> 00:08:30,380
So it's almost like being a detective, you know, looking for those clues that can unlock a deeper understanding.

173
00:08:30,700 --> 00:08:31,820
I love that analogy.

174
00:08:31,950 --> 00:08:35,600
I've definitely been in conversations where I could tell the other person wasn't really listening.

175
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:37,380
It's such a deflating feeling.

176
00:08:37,400 --> 00:08:41,390
It's disrespectful and it ultimately leads to a breakdown in communication.

177
00:08:41,670 --> 00:08:44,600
So how do we demonstrate that we're actively listening?

178
00:08:45,180 --> 00:08:48,520
Well, first and foremost, maintain eye contact.

179
00:08:48,840 --> 00:08:50,210
Right, but not in a creepy way.

180
00:08:50,230 --> 00:08:50,790
Of course not.

181
00:08:50,810 --> 00:08:59,100
It's about making natural eye contact, looking away occasionally to process information, and then returning your gaze to show that you're still with them.

182
00:08:59,280 --> 00:08:59,620
Got it.

183
00:09:00,040 --> 00:09:03,790
So what about other nonverbal cues, like nodding or tilting your head?

184
00:09:03,810 --> 00:09:04,550
Those are great, too.

185
00:09:04,570 --> 00:09:06,710
They convey interest and encouragement.

186
00:09:07,210 --> 00:09:09,740
It makes the other person feel heard and understood.

187
00:09:09,720 --> 00:09:12,980
And what about those little verbal cues like uh-huh or yeah?

188
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:17,430
Those can be helpful, but use them sparingly instead of just mindless fillers.

189
00:09:18,330 --> 00:09:22,030
Try to offer more substantial responses that show you're really engaged.

190
00:09:22,450 --> 00:09:32,500
So like if someone says, we're facing a real challenge with employee retention, instead of just saying, uh-huh, I could say something like, that sounds tough.

191
00:09:33,030 --> 00:09:36,130
What factors do you think are contributing to the high turnover?

192
00:09:36,160 --> 00:09:36,840
Exactly.

193
00:09:36,880 --> 00:09:41,490
You're acknowledging the problem, expressing empathy and inviting them to elaborate.

194
00:09:41,880 --> 00:09:45,830
And by asking a question, you're also subtly guiding the conversation.

195
00:09:45,990 --> 00:09:46,510
Precisely.

196
00:09:47,030 --> 00:09:54,160
It's about finding that balance between listening attentively and strategically steering the conversation towards a mutually beneficial outcome.

197
00:09:54,680 --> 00:09:55,340
This great stuff.

198
00:09:55,720 --> 00:09:59,580
One thing I struggle with is knowing when to interject with a comment or a question.

199
00:09:59,840 --> 00:10:03,370
I don't want to interrupt, but I also don't want to seem passive or disengaged.

200
00:10:03,460 --> 00:10:04,530
It's a common challenge.

201
00:10:04,550 --> 00:10:05,890
There's no hard and fast rule.

202
00:10:05,970 --> 00:10:13,360
It's about reading the situation, paying attention to the speaker's cues, and finding those natural pauses where you can seamlessly interject.

203
00:10:13,380 --> 00:10:14,140
Like a dance, right?

204
00:10:14,180 --> 00:10:15,690
Exactly, a conversational dance.

205
00:10:16,050 --> 00:10:19,720
Okay, so we've talked a lot about listening, but what about speaking?

206
00:10:20,400 --> 00:10:22,460
Ah, yes, the other half of the equation.

207
00:10:22,560 --> 00:10:25,710
What are some key things to keep in mind when we are the ones speaking?

208
00:10:25,890 --> 00:10:27,430
Well, clarity is paramount.

209
00:10:27,980 --> 00:10:32,530
Business communication is not about using fancy language or trying to impress people.

210
00:10:33,130 --> 00:10:38,640
It's about conveying your message in a clear, concise, and easily understandable way.

211
00:10:39,180 --> 00:10:40,420
So, no jargon.

212
00:10:40,580 --> 00:10:41,710
Key it to a minimum.

213
00:10:41,860 --> 00:10:44,680
I know I've been in those meetings where someone just goes on and on.

214
00:10:44,960 --> 00:10:47,240
And by the end, I have no idea what they were trying to say.

215
00:10:47,380 --> 00:10:48,340
It happens all the time.

216
00:10:48,380 --> 00:10:51,350
It's often because they're not clear about the purpose of their communication.

217
00:10:51,380 --> 00:10:56,830
Okay, so before you even start speaking, ask yourself, what's the key message I want to get across?

218
00:10:56,910 --> 00:10:57,610
Exactly.

219
00:10:57,670 --> 00:11:05,620
And once you know that, you can structure your message accordingly and make sure every word you say is driving towards that goal.

220
00:11:06,040 --> 00:11:08,280
It's like having a roadmap for the conversation.

221
00:11:08,470 --> 00:11:10,740
I like that, a conversational roadmap.

222
00:11:10,750 --> 00:11:14,590
And speaking of roadmaps, I came across this technique called signposting.

223
00:11:14,950 --> 00:11:15,450
Have you heard of that?

224
00:11:15,470 --> 00:11:15,890
Oh, yes.

225
00:11:16,010 --> 00:11:18,880
Signposting is a great way to guide the listener through your message.

226
00:11:18,910 --> 00:11:23,620
So it's like using verbal cues to signal transitions or highlight key points.

227
00:11:23,640 --> 00:11:24,040
Exactly.

228
00:11:24,080 --> 00:11:29,340
Like saying things like, OK, now that we've covered the challenges, let's move on to potential solutions.

229
00:11:29,360 --> 00:11:32,070
It helps the listeners stay engaged and follow your train of thought.

230
00:11:32,280 --> 00:11:32,890
Precisely.

231
00:11:33,130 --> 00:11:38,670
You know, it's amazing how these subtle techniques can have such a big impact on how we communicate.

232
00:11:38,770 --> 00:11:39,570
They really can.

233
00:11:39,610 --> 00:11:44,720
I'm starting to see conversation as more of a strategic skill than I ever did before.

234
00:11:44,950 --> 00:11:47,170
And that's a powerful shift in perspective.

235
